{
  "topic": "Why ice floats on water",
  "language": "en",
  "category": "ciencia",
  "title": "Why does ice float? 🧊",
  "hook": "Why does ice float, if it's just solid water?",
  "presenter": "perro",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "role": "hook",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "hie_hook",
      "narration": "Why does ice float, if it's just solid water?",
      "on_screen_text": "Why does ice float?",
      "highlight_words": [
        "ice"
      ],
      "sfx": "impact",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "hie_compare",
      "narration": "Almost every substance is denser as a solid, so it sinks in its own liquid. Water is the exception.",
      "on_screen_text": "Water is the exception",
      "highlight_words": [
        "exception"
      ],
      "sfx": "pop",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "hie_molecule",
      "narration": "Each water molecule has one oxygen and two hydrogens, shaped like a little letter V.",
      "on_screen_text": "H₂O: a little V",
      "highlight_words": [
        "V"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "hie_liquid",
      "narration": "In liquid water, the molecules move around and crowd together with no order.",
      "on_screen_text": "Liquid: crowded",
      "highlight_words": [
        "crowded"
      ],
      "sfx": "bubbles",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "hie_lattice",
      "narration": "But when it freezes, they lock into a network of hexagons that leaves gaps.",
      "on_screen_text": "Ice: hexagons with gaps",
      "highlight_words": [
        "gaps"
      ],
      "sfx": "riser",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "hie_expand",
      "narration": "Because of those gaps, ice takes up about nine percent more space than water, so it's lighter for its size.",
      "on_screen_text": "+9% volume",
      "highlight_words": [
        "+9%"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "hie_iceberg",
      "narration": "That's why it floats, but just barely: an iceberg hides about ninety percent underwater.",
      "on_screen_text": "90% hidden",
      "highlight_words": [
        "90%"
      ],
      "sfx": "whoosh",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "hie_lake",
      "narration": "And because of that, lakes freeze from the top down, and fish survive in the water below.",
      "on_screen_text": "Fish stay safe",
      "highlight_words": [
        "safe"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "role": "surprise",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "hie_snow",
      "narration": "That same hexagonal network is why snowflakes have six arms.",
      "on_screen_text": "That's why: 6 arms",
      "highlight_words": [
        "6"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "role": "closing",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "hie_outro",
      "narration": "Send this to someone who always wants extra ice.",
      "on_screen_text": "For the extra-ice crowd",
      "highlight_words": [
        "extra-ice"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Almost every substance is denser as a solid, but water isn't: when it freezes, its molecules form a hexagonal network with gaps, ice takes up ~9% more space and floats, with ~90% of an iceberg underwater. That's why lakes freeze from the top down and fish survive below, and why snowflakes have six arms. Original 3D animation; exaggerated scale.",
